function fs_new_content_guides_5d() {
    return [
    [
        'slug'       => 'roth-conversion-before-year-end',
        'title'      => 'Roth Conversion Before Year End: How Much to Convert',
        'keyword'    => 'roth conversion before year end',
        'desc'       => 'Roth conversion before year end 2026: the December 31 deadline, bracket-filling strategy, a worked example and why conversions cannot be undone.',
        'excerpt'    => 'How to decide how much of a traditional IRA or 401(k) to convert to a Roth IRA before December 31, 2026, using a bracket-filling strategy.',
        'answer'     => 'A Roth conversion must be completed by December 31 to count for that tax year, and once done it cannot be undone. A common approach is "bracket filling": converting just enough traditional IRA or 401(k) money to use up the rest of your current tax bracket without crossing into the next one, so the conversion is taxed at a rate you already know.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-03 09:00',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'A Roth conversion counts for a tax year only if completed by December 31 of that year, and it cannot be recharacterized (undone) afterward.',
            'The converted amount is taxed as ordinary income in the year of the conversion, on top of your other income.',
            'Bracket filling means converting only enough to use the room left in your current tax bracket before the next, higher bracket starts.',
            'A Roth conversion is not the same as a backdoor Roth contribution, and a large conversion can also raise Medicare premiums two years later through IRMAA.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">If you are weighing a <strong>Roth conversion before year end</strong>, the deadline that matters is December 31. A Roth conversion moves money from a traditional IRA, SEP IRA, SIMPLE IRA or old 401(k) into a Roth IRA, and the converted amount is taxed as ordinary income in the year the conversion happens. Unlike a Roth contribution, a conversion cannot be reversed once it is done. This guide walks through why the deadline is firm, how the "bracket filling" strategy works, a worked example using the 2026 tax brackets, and the Medicare premium issue many people overlook.</p>'
            . '<p><em>This guide is educational and reflects IRS rules as published. Tax software and a CPA can confirm the exact numbers for your return; some figures below, especially Medicare IRMAA thresholds, are estimates pending official confirmation.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>Why the December 31 deadline is a hard stop</h2><p>A Roth conversion must be completed inside the calendar tax year to count for that year. There is no extension: unlike an IRA contribution, which you can still make up until the April tax filing deadline for the prior year, a conversion has to be done, dollars moved, before December 31. If the conversion settles on January 2, it counts for the following tax year instead, whether you meant it to or not.</p>'
            . '<p>The deadline matters more now than it used to because of a change made by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. Before 2018, if you converted and then had a bad year in the markets, or simply changed your mind, you could "recharacterize" the conversion back into a traditional IRA by the extended filing deadline, effectively undoing it. According to the IRS, effective January 1, 2018, a conversion from a traditional IRA, SEP or SIMPLE to a Roth IRA can no longer be recharacterized. The same rule blocks undoing a rollover from a 401(k) or 403(b) into a Roth IRA. That means every Roth conversion you make now is final: if the account value drops right after you convert, you still owe tax on the amount you converted, not the lower value it fell to.</p>'
            . '<h2>How a Roth conversion is taxed</h2><p>A conversion adds the converted amount to your taxable income for the year, taxed at your ordinary income tax rates, the same brackets that apply to wages and interest. There is no special conversion rate. If you convert pre-tax traditional IRA money, generally the full amount is taxable; if your traditional IRA also has after-tax (nondeductible) contributions, a pro-rata calculation using IRS Form 8606 determines how much of the conversion is taxable and how much is a tax-free return of basis.</p>'
            . '<p>Because the conversion is just more ordinary income for the year, it can also push up income-based costs that are not part of your tax bracket at all, including the taxable portion of Social Security benefits, eligibility for certain tax credits, and, for Medicare enrollees, future Part B and Part D premiums (see the IRMAA section below).</p>'
            . '<h2>2026 federal income tax brackets</h2><p>Knowing where your income currently sits inside the bracket structure is the starting point for any year-end conversion decision. These are the 2026 tax year brackets.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Single filers, taxable income up to</th><th>Married filing jointly, taxable income up to</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>10%</td><td>$12,400</td><td>$24,800</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>12%</td><td>$50,400</td><td>$100,800</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>22%</td><td>$105,700</td><td>$211,400</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>24%</td><td>$201,775</td><td>$403,550</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>32%</td><td>$256,225</td><td>$512,450</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>35%</td><td>$640,600</td><td>$768,700</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>37%</td><td>above $640,600</td><td>above $768,700</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>The 2026 standard deduction is $16,100 for single filers and $32,200 for married couples filing jointly. These figures come from Rev. Proc. 2025-32; always confirm the exact thresholds for your filing status on IRS.gov before finalizing a conversion amount, since itemized deductions, credits and state taxes can change your effective bracket. For a broader year-end checklist, see our <a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">year-end tax planning checklist</a>, and estimate your bracket with the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What is "bracket filling"?</h2><p>Bracket filling is the core strategy behind most year-end Roth conversion decisions. The idea is simple: find how much taxable income room is left between your current income and the top of your current tax bracket, then convert up to that amount, no more. Converting within the bracket you are already in means the newly converted income is taxed at a rate you already know and have budgeted for. Convert past the line, and the extra dollars are taxed at the next, higher rate.</p>'
            . '<p>This works best for people whose income dips in a particular year, for example the first few years of retirement before Social Security and required withdrawals start, or a year with unusually low wages. Filling the 12% or 22% bracket with conversion income in a low-income year can move money into a Roth at a lower lifetime rate than it would be taxed later, when required minimum distributions or Social Security push income higher.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: filling the 22% bracket</h2><p>Assume a single filer in 2026 with $90,000 of taxable income after the standard deduction, no other changes expected before year-end. Based on the 2026 brackets above, the 22% bracket runs from $50,400 to $105,700 for a single filer. That leaves room to convert before crossing into the 24% bracket.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Taxable income before conversion</td><td>$90,000</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Top of the 22% bracket (single, 2026)</td><td>$105,700</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Room left in the 22% bracket</td><td>$15,700</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Roth conversion amount (bracket filling)</td><td>$15,700</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Federal tax on the conversion (at 22%)</td><td>about $3,454</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Taxable income after conversion</td><td>$105,700 (right at the top of the 22% bracket)</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>By converting exactly $15,700, this filer uses up the rest of the 22% bracket without pushing any income into the 24% bracket. Converting $20,000 instead would push $4,300 of that conversion into the 24% bracket, costing roughly $86 more in tax on that slice than if it had been converted in a future, lower-income year. The math changes with every other item on the return, deductions, credits, capital gains, state tax, so this example is illustrative, not a substitute for running your own numbers. Try the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> or the <a href="{tool:ira-calculator}">IRA calculator</a> with your actual figures before converting.</p>'
            . '<h2>The IRMAA wrinkle: a 2026 conversion can raise 2028 Medicare premiums</h2><p>If you are on Medicare or will be within a couple of years, a large year-end conversion deserves an extra look. Medicare Part B and Part D premiums include an income-related surcharge called IRMAA, and the amount is based on your tax return from two years earlier. That means a Roth conversion completed in 2026 flows into the income figure that could raise your Medicare premiums in 2028, not 2026 or 2027.</p>'
            . '<p><em>The current-year (2026) first-tier IRMAA thresholds are near $109,000 for single filers and $218,000 for married couples filing jointly, used here to illustrate the mechanism. These thresholds are adjusted annually, so confirm the actual figures that will apply to your 2028 premiums on medicare.gov or ssa.gov before converting a large amount.</em></p>'
            . '<p>Because IRMAA tiers work as cliffs, going one dollar over a threshold can trigger a full year of higher premiums for both spouses on Medicare, not just a prorated amount. If you are near a Medicare enrollment age, consider spreading a large conversion across several smaller year-end conversions over multiple years instead of one lump sum, so no single year’s income crosses an IRMAA threshold.</p>'
            . '<h2>The 5-year rule for converted amounts</h2><p>Converted money has its own 5-year clock that is separate from the 5-year rule for Roth earnings. If you withdraw converted principal before age 59 1/2 and before five years have passed since that specific conversion, the 10% early-withdrawal penalty can apply even though you already paid income tax on the conversion. Each year’s conversion starts its own 5-year clock, so someone doing several year-end conversions in different years is tracking several separate 5-year periods. This rule is about the penalty on early access, not about whether the conversion itself is taxable; that tax is already settled in the year you convert.</p>'
            . '<h2>Roth conversion vs. backdoor Roth: not the same thing</h2><p>A year-end Roth conversion is often confused with a "backdoor Roth," but they solve different problems. A Roth conversion moves existing traditional IRA or old 401(k) money, often built up over years, into a Roth IRA, and it is usually a strategic choice about which bracket to pay tax in. A backdoor Roth is a two-step move used mainly by higher earners who are blocked from contributing to a Roth IRA directly because their income is too high: they make a new, nondeductible contribution to a traditional IRA and then immediately convert that same contribution to a Roth. The backdoor method still uses a "conversion" as its second step, so the no-recharacterization and ordinary-income rules above apply to it too, but the dollars and goals are different. If you already have other traditional IRA balances, converting a backdoor contribution triggers the same pro-rata Form 8606 calculation described earlier.</p>'
            . '<h2>Step-by-step: deciding how much to convert before December 31</h2><ol><li><strong>Estimate your 2026 taxable income</strong> with all income sources already locked in, wages, interest, dividends, capital gains.</li><li><strong>Find the top of your current federal tax bracket</strong> from the table above for your filing status.</li><li><strong>Subtract to find your remaining bracket room.</strong> That is the ceiling for a bracket-filling conversion.</li><li><strong>Check state tax effects</strong>, since many states tax conversion income too, with their own brackets.</li><li><strong>Check Medicare status.</strong> If you or a spouse is enrolled in or nearing Medicare, weigh the IRMAA lookback before converting a large amount in one year.</li><li><strong>Submit the conversion with your custodian well before December 31</strong>, since processing can take several business days and brokerages often set earlier internal cutoffs in late December.</li><li><strong>Keep records</strong>, including Form 1099-R from the old account and Form 8606 if nondeductible contributions were involved, for your tax return.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes with year-end Roth conversions</h2><ul><li><strong>Waiting until the last few business days of December.</strong> Many brokerages require conversion requests several business days before December 31 to guarantee same-year settlement.</li><li><strong>Assuming you can undo it.</strong> Since the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act change, a completed conversion cannot be recharacterized back to a traditional IRA, even if the investments lose value right after.</li><li><strong>Ignoring the next bracket.</strong> Converting past the top of your current bracket taxes the excess at the higher rate, which can erase the benefit of bracket filling.</li><li><strong>Forgetting Medicare.</strong> A conversion two years before applying for Medicare, or while already enrolled, can raise premiums through IRMAA, even though the conversion itself has nothing to do with Medicare.</li><li><strong>Confusing a conversion with a backdoor Roth contribution</strong>, which changes the planning goal and the dollar amounts involved.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>For more on managing taxable income near bracket edges, see our guide to <a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">tax-loss harvesting</a>, and for a broader view of retirement balances, our <a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">retirement planning guide</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">Year-end tax planning checklist</a></li><li><a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">Tax-loss harvesting explained</a></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/ira-faqs-recharacterization-of-ira-contributions" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRA FAQs: Recharacterization of IRA Contributions</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/roth-iras" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Roth IRAs</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/topic-no-557-additional-tax-on-early-distributions-from-traditional-and-roth-iras" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Topic no. 557, additional tax on early distributions</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8606" rel="noopener" target="_blank">About Form 8606</a>. 2026 tax bracket and standard deduction figures are drawn from Rev. Proc. 2025-32 as reported by secondary coverage; confirm against the Revenue Procedure itself on IRS.gov. IRMAA mechanics and threshold figures are drawn from secondary advisory sources and are not independently confirmed against a CMS or SSA primary page; verify current thresholds at medicare.gov before relying on them.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'Can I still do a Roth conversion before year end?', 'Yes, as long as your financial institution can complete the conversion, moving the money out of the traditional account and into the Roth, by December 31 of the tax year. Many brokerages set internal deadlines several business days before year-end to guarantee same-year processing, so submit the request early rather than in the final days of December.' ],
            [ 'Can I undo a Roth conversion if I change my mind?', 'No. Since January 1, 2018, under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, the IRS no longer allows a Roth conversion to be recharacterized back into a traditional IRA. Once the conversion is complete, it is final, and you owe ordinary income tax on the converted amount even if the investments lose value afterward.' ],
            [ 'How much should I convert to a Roth IRA before December 31?', 'A common approach is "bracket filling": estimate your taxable income for the year, find the top of your current federal tax bracket, and convert only enough to use up the remaining room in that bracket. Converting beyond that point pushes the extra amount into the next, higher tax rate.' ],
            [ 'Is a Roth conversion taxed differently from regular income?', 'No. The converted amount is added to your other taxable income for the year and taxed at your regular federal (and usually state) income tax rates. There is no separate, lower rate for conversion income, which is why timing a conversion to a low-income year or a bracket with room left matters.' ],
            [ 'Does a Roth conversion affect Medicare premiums?', 'It can. Medicare premiums use a 2-year lookback, so a conversion done in 2026 could affect your Medicare Part B and Part D premiums in 2028 through the income-related surcharge known as IRMAA. The exact thresholds for 2028 were not available at the time of writing, so check medicare.gov or ssa.gov before converting a large amount if you are near or on Medicare.' ],
            [ 'Is a Roth conversion the same as a backdoor Roth contribution?', 'No. A Roth conversion moves existing traditional IRA or old 401(k) savings into a Roth IRA, usually chosen to manage which tax bracket the money is taxed in. A backdoor Roth is a two-step move for people whose income is too high to contribute to a Roth directly: a new nondeductible traditional IRA contribution is made and then converted. Both steps are taxed under the same conversion rules.' ],
            [ 'What happens if I withdraw converted money before age 59 1/2?', 'Each conversion starts its own 5-year clock. If you withdraw the converted principal before that 5-year period ends and before you turn 59 1/2, the 10% early-withdrawal penalty can apply, even though you already paid income tax on the conversion itself. The 5-year clock for converted amounts is tracked separately from the 5-year rule for Roth earnings.' ],
            [ 'Do I need to file anything special after a Roth conversion?', 'Your IRA custodian reports the conversion on Form 1099-R, and you report it on your federal tax return. If your traditional IRA included nondeductible (after-tax) contributions, you also need Form 8606 to calculate how much of the conversion is taxable versus a tax-free return of basis.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
